{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"brokendown-moonlit-kiskadee","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\n`rebar transition` **silently accepts and ignores unknown flags**. Demonstrated on a real ticket,\nno mutation:\n\n```\n$ rebar transition major-specified-bream open open --definitely-not-a-real-flag\nno change: 50e3-6d2a-0e7f-4e92 already open\n```\n\nNo error, no warning, exit 0. A misspelled or unsupported flag does nothing and says nothing.\n\n## Why this is more than a nit: `--caused-by` is documented but not implemented on the CLI\n\n`rebar transition --help` lists only `[--class=<value>] [--reason=<text>] [--force] [--force=<reason>] [--output json]`. There is no `--caused-by`. But it is instructed in at least three places:\n\n- **`docs/user-guide.md:191-194`** — \"explicitly with `--caused-by <id>` (which overrides the\n  git-blame auto-derivation)\", with a worked example:\n  `rebar transition <bug> in_progress closed --class=regression --caused-by=<culprit-id>`\n- **`docs/clone-guidance.md:15,18`** — \"Supply `--caused-by` when an explicit link is preferable.\"\n- **The `/rebar-debug` skill** (lines ~498-512) makes it mandatory: *\"`--caused-by` on the close is\n  not optional once you have an origin, even if you already linked one\"*, and *\"Pass the flag on\n  the close every time.\"*\n\nThe library side exists — `_lib_writes.py:339` threads `caused_by=`, and\n`transition_close.py:175` defines `_apply_caused_by` — so this is a **CLI surface gap**, not a\nmissing capability.\n\n**Combined with the silent-ignore behaviour, the effect is that every agent following the\ndocumented protocol believes it supplied a proven attribution while the system actually fell back\nto blame.** The instruction and the outcome diverge, silently, on the exact code path whose purpose\nis to make attribution trustworthy.\n\n## Observed consequences, this session\n\nFour bug closes today passed `--caused-by=<proven-origin>` per the skill. In each case:\n\n- the flag was ignored;\n- the `caused_by` edge that existed came from an earlier explicit `rebar link`;\n- blame auto-derivation ran on close and attached a SECOND edge.\n\nThat mis-diagnosis was filed as `ruling-magnific-bats` (`10d0-c26c-bdbb-4b1f`) with the wrong\npremise — \"`--caused-by` adds a duplicate edge\" — when the actual mechanism is \"blame\nauto-derivation adds an edge without checking whether one is already recorded\". A correction is\nrecorded on that ticket.\n\nWorth noting in fairness: in the observed cases **blame landed on the same origin the agent had\nproven by hand**, so the duplicate was redundant rather than wrong. That is evidence about blame's\naccuracy, not an argument that the gap is harmless — an agent that believes it overrode the\nfallback has no way to discover that it did not.\n\n## Two defects, and they should probably be fixed together\n\n1. **Unknown flags are silently ignored.** A typo (`--forse`, `--clas=regression`) does nothing and\n   reports success. Rejecting unknown flags is the standard contract and would have surfaced this\n   in seconds.\n2. **`--caused-by` is documented and instructed but absent from the CLI.** Either implement it\n   (the library already supports it) or remove it from `docs/user-guide.md`, `docs/clone-guidance.md`\n   and the `/rebar-debug` skill. Implementing looks strictly better: the whole point of the\n   attribution step is that a proven origin beats a guess.\n\nFixing (1) without (2) converts a silent failure into a loud one — an improvement, but it would\nbreak every documented invocation and every agent following the protocol. Fixing (2) without (1)\nleaves the next typo silent. Consider ordering deliberately.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n1. `rebar transition <any-open-ticket> open open --definitely-not-a-real-flag` → `no change`, exit 0.\n2. `rebar transition --help` → no `--caused-by` in the usage line.\n3. `grep -n 'caused-by' docs/user-guide.md` → documented, with an example command.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An unknown flag passed to `rebar transition` is REJECTED with a message naming the flag, not\n      silently ignored — verified by a test asserting a non-zero exit and the flag name in the error.\n- [ ] The unknown-flag rejection covers the other mutating verbs too, or a reasoned decision is\n      recorded for why `transition` is special — verified per verb touched.\n- [ ] `--caused-by=<id>` on a bug close either works end-to-end (the recorded edge is the supplied\n      origin, and blame auto-derivation does NOT also run) or is removed from\n      `docs/user-guide.md`, `docs/clone-guidance.md` and the `/rebar-debug` skill. Decide and test\n      whichever is chosen.\n- [ ] If implemented: supplying `--caused-by` suppresses the blame fallback entirely, so exactly\n      one edge results — verified by a test, and coordinated with `ruling-magnific-bats` (1973),\n      which makes the `_apply_caused_by` write idempotent.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check`\n      (`new=0 increased=0 stale=0`) pass.\n\n## Provenance\n\nFound while verifying a subagent's report that `rebar transition` has no `--caused-by` flag. The\nagent was right and I had been asserting otherwise all session, including in instructions to other\nagents.","id":"0276-7eea-baf0-48e3","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"rebar transition silently ignores unknown flags, and the documented --caused-by is absent from the CLI"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787270402331197001,"uuid":"6eb9a5b3-e2ec-4a08-8da5-fbce3533e86c"}